   ┗┳━┳━┳━┳┛    ESP32 series emulator · v0.51.0
    ╹ ╹ ╹ ╹     https://github.com/espressif/esp-emulator

RISC-V emulator for ESP32-C3/C6/P4 IDF binaries

Usage: esp-emu [OPTIONS] --chip <CHIP> --firmware <FIRMWARE>

Options:
      --chip <CHIP>
          Target chip
          
          [possible values: esp32c3, esp32c5, esp32c6, esp32h2, esp32p4,
          esp32s3, esp32s31]

      --firmware <FIRMWARE>
          Path to firmware binary (merged flash image)
          
          Download mode (--strap-mode 0x02/0x04) never boots firmware, so only
          there may this flag be omitted; the ROM loader then sees a blank flash
          (esptool/espefuse still work).

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Networking:
      --net <NET>
          Network backend: "user", "tap,ifname=tap0", "vmnet"
          
          "user" is slirp-style with no host setup. When omitted, Linux tries
          tap0 first and falls back to user; other OSes default to user.

      --wifi-ssid <WIFI_SSID>
          WiFi soft AP SSID (default: myssid)

      --wifi-password <WIFI_PASSWORD>
          WiFi passphrase (default: mypassword)
          
          Must be 8-63 chars. Pass "" for an open (unencrypted) AP.

      --wifi-fw-ap-password <PASS>
          Password of the firmware's own soft AP, used when the emulator joins
          it (default: --wifi-password). Pass "" for an open AP

      --wifi-auth <MODE>
          Soft AP authentication: wpa2-wpa3, wpa3-sae, wpa2-psk, open
          
          Default is wpa2-wpa3 (transition: PSK and SAE offered, PMF capable),
          which lets WPA2 and WPA3 firmware each negotiate what it supports; ""
          as --wifi-password means open. Pin wpa3-sae (PMF required) or wpa2-psk
          to test a firmware's security policy against that AP.

      --wifi-radius <HOST:PORT,SECRET>
          RADIUS server for the enterprise modes: HOST:PORT,SECRET
          
          EAP from the station is relayed to it (hostapd's EAP server or
          FreeRADIUS); the MSK it returns becomes the PMK.

      --wifi-sae-pwe <PWE>
          SAE password element: both, h2e, hunt-and-peck (default: both)
          
          both and h2e advertise hash-to-element (RSNXE); hunt-and-peck hides it
          so a station falls back to the looping derivation.

BLE:
      --elf <PATH>...
          Application ELF(s) for BLE symbol interception (e.g.,
          build/project.elf). Takes several paths, or may be repeated: at app
          start the ELF whose SHA-256 the running app's descriptor records is
          used, so after an OTA the new build's ELF takes over

      --ble-hci <BLE_HCI>
          HCI backend: "tcp:host:port" for Bumble, "hci0" for Linux adapter

Thread:
      --thread-sim <THREAD_SIM>
          802.15.4 bridge spec, e.g. "bind:9001,peer:127.0.0.1:9002"
          
          Forwards frames between the emulated IEEE 802.15.4 driver and a UDP
          peer (another emulator, or OpenThread POSIX simulation).

ESP-Hosted:
      --hosted <HOSTED>
          ESP-Hosted SDIO bridge: bridge:host:/sock or bridge:slave:/sock
          
          `bridge:host:/path/to/sock` — P4 host instance, connects to slave.
          `bridge:slave:/path/to/sock` — C6 slave instance, listens for host.
          Start the slave first, then the host. Lossless Unix stream.

      --hosted-reset-gpio <PIN>
          P4 GPIO wired to the C6 EN/CHIP_PU reset pin (host side only).
          
          When the host firmware pulses this GPIO (the ESP-Hosted slave-reset
          sequence), a RESET is forwarded over the bridge and the slave instance
          reboots — modelling the reset wire, which is not part of the SDIO bus.
          Must match the firmware's reset-GPIO Kconfig.

      --hosted-reset-active-high
          Treat the slave-reset GPIO as active-high (default: active-low / EN)

ROM:
      --rom <ROM>
          Path to ROM ELF file (e.g., esp32c3_rev3_rom.elf)
          
          When omitted, a default ROM embedded at build time is used for the
          selected --chip.

Advanced:
      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Exit after this duration (e.g., "5s", "10s")

      --exit-on <EXIT_ON>
          Exit with code 0 when UART output contains this string

      --save-state
          Save flash/NVS state to disk on exit (overwrites firmware file)

      --efuse <EFUSE>
          Path to eFuse binary file (QEMU-compatible, 336 bytes)

      --skip-bootloader
          Skip bootloader: load app directly from partition table

      --skip-rom
          Skip the mask ROM; reset the CPU directly at the bootloader entry
          
          Default executes the real ROM, which is required for
          KEY_SOURCE_KEY_MGR flash recovery and other eFuse-driven cold-boot
          decisions.

      --batch-size <BATCH_SIZE>
          Batch size, instructions per iteration (default: 50000)

      --inject <INJECT>
          Payload to inject into UART RX (paired with --inject-on)
          
          Supports \n / \r escapes. Repeatable: each --inject is paired
          positionally with the matching --inject-on trigger and fires once.

      --inject-on <INJECT_ON>
          Trigger string in UART TX output that fires the paired --inject
          
          Repeatable; each --inject-on is paired positionally with --inject.

      --uart-tcp <HOST:PORT>
          Bridge UART0 to a TCP server (e.g. "127.0.0.1:5555")
          
          Mirrors QEMU's `-serial tcp::PORT,server,nowait`. esptool can connect
          via `socket://127.0.0.1:5555`. While active, UART0 RX comes from the
          socket (not stdin) and UART0 TX goes to the socket (not stdout). A
          USB-Serial/JTAG console (see --console) still prints to stdout. With
          --strap-mode 0x04 the socket is the USB-Serial/JTAG download port.

      --uart1-tcp <HOST:PORT>
          Bridge UART1 to a TCP server (e.g. "127.0.0.1:5556")
          
          Side channel for simulating an external serial device (sensor, GPS,
          modem) attached to UART1. Independent of --uart-tcp: stdin/stdout and
          --exit-on / --inject-on keep tracking UART0. Without a connected
          client, UART1 TX is discarded (like an unattached chardev in QEMU).

      --control-tcp <HOST:PORT>
          Serve a host control channel on this address (e.g. "127.0.0.1:5556")
          
          Newline-delimited commands, one reply per line:
          
          - `reset` — chip reset, what a DTR/RTS toggle does on hardware, which
          a UART socket cannot express because pyserial's `socket://` ignores
          modem control lines. `reset --soft` gives a software reset instead. -
          `efuse-load <path>` — read a QEMU-compatible eFuse image into the
          running machine and reset it, so bits burned by a download-mode
          instance take effect without restarting the process. - `erase-region
          <offset> <length>` and `erase-flash` — what `esptool erase-region` /
          `erase-flash` do, applied to the running machine's flash so the
          firmware sees it without a reload. - `write-region <offset> <file>` —
          as `esptool write-flash`, reading the data from a path because the
          channel is line based. - `ping` — answers `ok`.

      --gdb <PORT>
          Serve a GDB remote stub on this TCP port (QEMU's `-s` is port 1234)
          
          riscv32-esp-elf-gdb connects with `target remote :PORT`. Supports
          register/memory read+write, software breakpoints, single-step, and
          continue — enough for scripted fault injection (breakpoint + `set
          var=...` + continue). Pair with --gdb-halt to mirror QEMU's `-s -S`.

      --gdb-halt
          Halt the CPU at the reset vector until a GDB client connects (QEMU -S)

      --no-panic-intercept
          With --elf, don't stop at the first firmware panic: let the firmware's
          own panic handler run, so it reboots or halts per its sdkconfig.
          
          For firmware that panics on purpose (a task-watchdog reset test, a
          deliberate abort()). Symbolicated backtraces and BLE interception
          stay.

      --trace <FILE>
          Write an execution trace to FILE in Chrome Trace Event format. Open it
          in https://ui.perfetto.dev (or chrome://tracing) for a per-task
          timeline, or serve the summary tables with `python3
          tools/trace-view.py FILE`. Needs --elf: the FreeRTOS `pxCurrentTCBs`
          symbol is what makes context switches visible

      --trace-pc-period <N>
          Sample the PC every N trace ticks (1 tick = 256 cycles) to build the
          hot-function table. 0 disables PC sampling
          
          [default: 16]

      --strap-mode <HEX>
          GPIO_STRAP register value at reset (hex 0x.. or decimal)
          
          Mirrors QEMU's `-global
          driver=esp32c3.gpio,property=strap_mode,value=`. 0x02 (UART0 download)
          or 0x04 (USB-Serial/JTAG download, via joint download) resets to ROM
          entry instead of firmware entry — pair with --uart-tcp so
          esptool/espefuse can drive it via `socket://`. For 0x04, run esptool
          with ESPTOOL_USB_VID_PID=303a:1001 so it detects the USB-JTAG
          transport.

      --psram-size <SIZE>
          External RAM the board is modelled with: 4M/8M/16M/32M/64M, or 0 for
          none
          
          Only the chips with external RAM take it (C5, P4, S3, S31). Firmware
          cannot choose: `esp_psram` has no size option for P4/S31 and reads the
          size out of the device's mode registers, so a 32 MB board is only 32
          MB here if the emulated die says so. `0` reports no die at all, which
          is the `CONFIG_SPIRAM_IGNORE_NOTFOUND` path. C5's quad device names
          2M-32M instead of 4M-64M. S3 takes either family
          (`CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_QUAD` or `_OCT`) so it names 2M-64M; a size only
          one family can express fits that die and reports the other absent.

      --console <CONSOLE>
          Console that stdin/stdout (and --exit-on/--inject-on) follow
          
          auto: UART0 until the firmware selects USB-Serial/JTAG in the ROM
          console selector (CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG builds), then
          EP1. A firmware with no ROM UART printer (CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_NONE with
          the USB-Serial/JTAG secondary) shows EP1 beside UART0, with input on
          UART0. IDF's default "UART0 + USB-Serial/JTAG secondary" prints once.
          uart0: always UART0, EP1 output discarded. usb-jtag: EP1 from the
          first byte, UART0 output discarded.
          
          [possible values: auto, uart0, usb-jtag]

      --i2c-dev <PORT:ADDR[,OPTS]>
          I2C device to answer on a port:
          PORT:ADDR[,width=1|2][,fill=0xNN][,reg=REG=VAL]...
          
          Puts a register-file device at 7-bit address ADDR on HP I2C port PORT
          (IDF `i2c_port_t`), beside the built-in EEPROM at 0x50. It ACKs,
          stores what is written and reads it back — enough for an IO expander,
          touch controller, PMIC or sensor whose driver only needs the
          transaction to succeed. `width` is the register-address size in bytes
          (default 1; 2 for 16-bit-register devices such as CST9xxx touch),
          `fill` the value every register starts at (default 0xFF), and
          `reg=REG=VAL` preloads one register, e.g. a chip-ID the driver checks.
          Repeat the flag for each device: `--i2c-dev 0:0x20 --i2c-dev
          0:0x5a,width=2,fill=0 --i2c-dev 0:0x34,reg=0x03=0x4a`.

      --rmt-loopback <TX:RX>
          RMT loopback pairs: TX_CH:RX_CH[,TX_CH:RX_CH...] (absolute channels)
          
          Feeds a TX channel's transmitted symbols into an RX channel's input
          stream — the data equivalent of wiring the two pins together. Channel
          numbers are the TRM's absolute indices: RX channels start at 2 on
          C3/C6/H2 and at 4 on P4/S31 (e.g. `--rmt-loopback 0:2` loops TX ch0 to
          RX ch2 on a C3). Lets e.g. the IDF ir_nec_transceiver example receive
          its own transmission. The looped waveform is the pre-carrier base
          signal, matching what a demodulating IR receiver outputs.

      --log-color <WHEN>
          Colorize firmware UART logs (default: auto; auto/always/never)
          
          Wraps lines starting with `I (..)`, `W (..)`, `E (..)` in IDF's
          esp_log palette as they pass through to stdout. `auto` enables it only
          when stdout is a TTY and `NO_COLOR` is unset. Bridged --uart-tcp
          output is never colorized.

Subcommands:
  esp-emu update      Self-update from GitHub Releases (try `update --help`)
  esp-emu --version   Print version and exit

[reconfirmation] no device/plugin registration API in the distributed binary.
